In .NET 10 (MAUI), `WindowSoftInputModeAdjust.Resize` was deprecated/removed because Android itself deprecated `SOFT_INPUT_ADJUST_RESIZE`. The recommended approach is now **edge-to-edge display with `WindowInsets` handling**.

## Solution

In your `Platforms/Android/MainActivity.cs`:

```csharp
using Android.Views;
using AndroidX.Core.View;

protected override void OnCreate(Bundle? savedInstanceState)
{
    base.OnCreate(savedInstanceState);

    // Enable edge-to-edge
    WindowCompat.SetDecorFitsSystemWindows(Window, false);

    // Listen for keyboard (IME) insets
    ViewCompat.SetOnApplyWindowInsetsListener(Window.DecorView, new InsetsListener());
}

private class InsetsListener : Java.Lang.Object, IOnApplyWindowInsetsListener
{
    public WindowInsetsCompat OnApplyWindowInsets(Android.Views.View v, WindowInsetsCompat insets)
    {
        var imeInsets = insets.GetInsets(WindowInsetsCompat.Type.Ime());
        var navInsets = insets.GetInsets(WindowInsetsCompat.Type.NavigationBars());

        // Pad the bottom by whichever is larger: keyboard or nav bar
        v.SetPadding(0, 0, 0, Math.Max(imeInsets.Bottom, navInsets.Bottom));

        return insets;
    }
}
```

## Key points

- **`SetDecorFitsSystemWindows(Window, false)`** opts into edge-to-edge, giving your app control over insets.
- **`WindowInsetsCompat.Type.Ime()`** reports the keyboard height.
- The padding pushes your content (including the input bar) above the keyboard.

If you prefer a XAML-only workaround without platform code, you can set `SoftInputMode = SoftInput.AdjustPan` in `MainActivity` — but that scrolls the entire view rather than resizing it, which may not look ideal for a chat screen.
